5 4. Target Strategic Micronutrients
Vitamin D3 & K2: Vitamin D acts as a master regulator for immune cells, helping them distinguish
between harmless signals and real threats. Check your blood levels to maintain an optimal range
(typically 40 to 60 ng/mL) [cite: 1].
Zinc and Magnesium: Essential cofactors for hundreds of enzymatic reactions, including the
creation and activation of immune cells. Get them through foods like pumpkin seeds, shellfish,
dark leafy greens, and grass-fed meats, supplementing only if dietary gaps exist [cite: 1].
Polyphenol-Rich Foods: Eat brightly colored berries, dark leafy greens, green tea, and extra
virgin olive oil. These compounds act as antioxidants, protecting your immune cells from oxidative
stress [cite: 1].
5. Mitigate Chronic Stress
Lower Cortisol Load: Chronic psychological stress floods the body with cortisol, which
over time suppresses immune function and exhausts your natural defenses. Implement
daily stress-buffering practices like breathwork, cold plunges, sauna sessions, or
dedicated downtime [cite: 1].
